<h2>The contract and the overrun</h2>
<p>The April 2006 agreement — contract price Rs. 189.63 crore — required completion by 26 November 2008; the work finished on 5 December 2009. NHAI granted the 374-day extension of time without penalty or liquidated damages, a fact that anchored everything that followed: an employer who extends time without fault findings against the contractor concedes, at least prima facie, that the delay was not the contractor&rsquo;s. The contractor claimed Rs. 40.33 crore under seven heads; the majority of the three-member tribunal awarded Rs. 15.80 crore across four — input costs (POL, cement, steel, bitumen), extended plant and equipment, and site and head-office overheads — while rejecting financing costs, bonus and loss of profit-earning capacity.</p>

<h2>The challenge, head by head</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Claim head</th><th>NHAI&rsquo;s attack</th><th>Why it failed</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Inputs — Rs. 4,38,36,642</td><td>Clause 70 price adjustment was the exclusive remedy</td><td>The award was for overrun-period costs under clauses 6.4, 12.2 and 42.2, net of Clause 70 receipts</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Plant &amp; equipment — Rs. 4,55,10,280</td><td>Wrong pro-rata method, aged machinery</td><td>The tribunal rejected the higher alternative computation and awarded only claimed ownership cost; quantification is its domain (McDermott)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Overheads — Rs. 6,86,95,108</td><td>No timely notice, no verifiable records, excessive</td><td>MoRTH Data Book rate of 11.64% less 15% mitigation held rationally supported (Batliboi)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Interest — 10% compounded monthly</td><td>Sub-clause 60.8 inapplicable; rate excessive</td><td>Within the tribunal&rsquo;s power; no contractual contradiction shown</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The standard applied</h2>
<p>The judgment works squarely within Ssangyong Engineering v. NHAI, (2019) 15 SCC 131: patent illegality and public policy, not appellate correction. The Court put the discipline plainly — <em>&ldquo;The question under Section 34 is not whether this Court would have reached the same findings.&rdquo;</em> On the Engineer&rsquo;s contemporaneous rejection of the claims, it added a point of wider use: the Engineer&rsquo;s view is material the tribunal must weigh, but <em>&ldquo;the Court must assess those reasons under Section 34, rather than treat the Engineer&rsquo;s rejection as conclusive.&rdquo;</em> Finding the majority&rsquo;s view plausible on every surviving head, it held: <em>&ldquo;The petition under Section 34 of the Act is accordingly dismissed&rdquo;</em> — the award stands unmodified, with no order as to costs.</p>

<h2>What employers and contractors should note</h2>
<p>Three practice points emerge. First, an unconditional extension of time is the single most consequential document in a prolongation dispute; employers who intend to contest causation should say so when granting it. Second, notice objections under clauses like 53.1&ndash;53.3 must be developed before the tribunal with specificity, because Section 34 review will not re-open them as fresh defences. Third, for contractors, the heads that failed — financing costs, bonus, loss of profitability — failed for want of proof, not of principle: the tribunal&rsquo;s willingness to award formula-based overheads did not extend to speculative heads. Eleven years after the award, the money remains payable with compounding interest — a reminder that an unsuccessful Section 34 petition is often the most expensive filing in the file.</p>
<div class="note">Section 34 proceedings test an award against statutory grounds; they do not re-appreciate evidence. Figures are those recorded in the judgment dated 23 November 2026.</div>
